⚙️ Lambda Expressions

Anonymous functions that can be treated as objects.

Implement functional interfaces (SAM – Single Abstract Method).

Concise functional-style coding.

Example:

@FunctionalInterface
interface Calculator { int calculate(int a, int b); }

Calculator add = (a, b) -> a + b;
System.out.println(add.calculate(2, 3)); // Output: 5


Anonymous Function:

A function not bound to a name.

Can access variables from enclosing scope.

Lambda expressions are anonymous functions.

⚙️ Method References

Shorter syntax for lambdas that just call an existing method.

Refers to a method without executing it immediately.

Example:

List<Integer> nums = Arrays.asList(1, 2, 3);
nums.forEach(System.out::println); // Equivalent to nums.forEach(n -> System.out.println(n));


Types:

Static method → ClassName::staticMethod

Instance method (specific object) → objRef::instanceMethod

Instance method (any object of class) → ClassName::instanceMethod

Constructor → ClassName::new

Key Point:

Lambda: explicitly calls the method
Method Reference: just points to the method; Java passes parameters automatically

⚙️ Functional Interfaces

Interface with one abstract method.

Can have any number of default/static methods.

@FunctionalInterface annotation recommended.

Common Types:

Interface	Input	Output	Use
Predicate	Yes	Boolean	Filtering/conditions
Consumer	Yes	None	Perform action
Function	Yes	Yes	Transform input → output
Supplier	None	Yes	Supply values

Example:

Predicate<Integer> isEven = n -> n % 2 == 0;
System.out.println(isEven.test(4)); // true

⚙️ Default & Static Methods in Interfaces

Interfaces can have method bodies with default or static.

Example:

interface Vehicle {
    default void start() { System.out.println("Starting..."); }
    static void stop() { System.out.println("Stopped."); }
}

⚙️ Stream API

Functional-style operations on collections.

Supports: map(), flatMap(), filter(), reduce(), collect(), etc.

Lazy evaluation, parallel processing possible.

Max Example:

int max = Stream.of(1,24,12,15,2)
                .max(Comparator.comparing(Integer::valueOf))
                .get();


Distinct Example:

List<Integer> li = someList.stream()
                           .distinct()
                           .collect(Collectors.toList());


map() vs flatMap():

map() → transform single element → single result

flatMap() → transform + flatten nested elements

Example:

// map()
List<String> cities = employees.stream()
                               .map(e -> e.getCity())
                               .collect(Collectors.toList());

// flatMap()
Set<String> allCities = employees.stream()
                                 .flatMap(e -> e.getCities().stream())
                                 .collect(Collectors.toSet());

⚙️ forEach()

Default method in Iterable and Stream.

Accepts a Consumer functional interface.

Example:

list.forEach(item -> System.out.println(item));

⚙️ Optional Class

Represents a value that may or may not be present.

Helps avoid NullPointerException.

Example:

Optional<String> name = Optional.ofNullable(null);
System.out.println(name.orElse("Default Name")); // Default Name

⚙️ New Date & Time API

Packages: LocalDate, LocalTime, LocalDateTime, ZonedDateTime

Replaces Date and Calendar (thread-safe, well-designed, supports zones).

Example:

LocalDate date = LocalDate.now();
LocalDateTime dateTime = LocalDateTime.now();

⚙️ Nashorn JavaScript Engine

Java 8 introduced JS engine inside Java.

Allows executing JavaScript code in Java applications.

⚙️ Base64 Encode/Decode

Built-in utility in java.util.Base64.

Example:

String encoded = Base64.getEncoder().encodeToString("Hello".getBytes());
System.out.println(encoded);


