==================== FUNCTIONAL INTERFACE & COMPARATOR ====================

1️⃣ Functional Interface Rule
- Must have exactly one abstract method.
- Can have default, static, or private methods (do not count as abstract).

2️⃣ Comparator as Functional Interface
- Single abstract method: compare(T o1, T o2)
- Default/static methods allowed:
      - static Comparator.comparingInt(ToIntFunction keyExtractor)
      - default Comparator<T> reversed()
      - etc.
- These helper methods do NOT violate functional interface rule.

3️⃣ How comparingInt works
- Example:
      Comparator<Employee> cmp = Comparator.comparingInt(e -> e.getSalary());
- What happens:
      1. comparingInt() is a static helper → returns a Comparator object
      2. Lambda (e -> e.getSalary()) provides implementation for the single abstract method compare()
      3. Default/static methods are just utilities → they do not count as abstract

4️⃣ Key Insight
- Functional interface ≠ only 1 method in total
- Only 1 abstract method is required
- Default/static methods can exist as helpers
- Lambda only implements the single abstract method

5️⃣ Analogy
- Comparator = blueprint
- compare() = mandatory abstract method → lambda fills this
- comparingInt(), reversed(), etc. = optional helpers
- Allows concise, readable code without breaking functional interface rules
